  ],
  "text": "Look at the solar system edge on, and you would almost miss it. You probably picture planets scattered around the sun in every direction. But all eight orbit inside the same thin sheet, barely tilted at all. Eight separate worlds picking the same plane by accident is not something accidents do. The sun and its planets grew out of one enormous cloud of gas and dust, slowly turning. As it collapsed, anything falling upward met something falling down, and both motions cancelled out. But sideways turning has nothing to cancel against, so only the spin survived. So the cloud had no choice but to flatten into a disc, and the planets formed inside it. Which is why Saturn's rings are flat, and why every spiral galaxy is too. That thin sliver you almost missed is the shape the solar system was born in. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
